          %p
            Fundamental physics is all based on the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+principle"} gauge principle
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+field"} gauge field
              configurations
            (discussed in detail below in
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#Fields"} Fields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equality"} equal
            or not. Instead it makes sense to ask if there is (or not) a
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+transformation"} gauge transformation
            from one to the other that exibits an gauge
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            between the two fields. The simplest example of this is described in detail below in
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#GaugeTransformationsInElectromagnetism"} Gauge transformations in electromagnetism
          %p
            But this means that the collection of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+fields"} gauge fields
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/mapping+space"} mapping space
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mi B
                    %msub
                      %mi G
                      %mi conn
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [X, \mathbf{B}G_{conn}]
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+space"} smooth space
            as considered above, for if it were such a smooth space, then we could ask if two gauge fields
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo ∇
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mo ∇
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi conn
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla_1, \nabla_2 \colon * \to [X,\mathbf{B}G_{conn}]
            were equal or not.
          %p
            Notice that this already applies to a single gauge field: given any
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mo *
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mi B
                  %msub
                    %mi G
                    %mi conn
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla \colon * \to [X,\mathbf{B}G_{conn}]
            it is certainly equal to itself, but is nevertheless also
            %em gauge equivalent
            to itself, but the latter it may be in several non-equivalent ways: there may be non-trivial auto-gauge transformations
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                    %mo →
                    %mo ∇
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla \to \nabla
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#Groups"} Groups
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            is itself the trivial gauge field,
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∇
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mo ∇
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla = \nabla_0
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+group"} gauge group
            of the given
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi Aut
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∇
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mo ∇
                    %mn 0
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mo ≃
                  %msub
                    %mo ∇
                    %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  G \simeq Aut(\nabla_0) \simeq \{\nabla_0 \stackrel{\simeq}{\to} \nabla_0\}
                  \\,.
          %p
            For this reason, the collection of
            %em all
            gauge fields and all gauge transformations between them form something that is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            over ever fixed element, but which generalizes the notion of a group in that there are not only auto-equivalences, but also equivalences going from one element to another. Such a structure is called a
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
            with smooth structure, a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/smooth+space"} smooth space
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoid"} groupoid
